1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,350
Chapter five. Video game cards.

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:12,741
May, nineteen ninety-seven. IBM's Deep Blue computer defeats world chess champion Garry Kasparov.

3
00:00:12,891 --> 00:00:18,055
It is a milestone, but it doesn't learn: it calculates millions of moves by brute force.

4
00:00:18,205 --> 00:00:28,241
The real breakthrough arrives from somewhere else. In two thousand nine, Fei-Fei Li and her team launch ImageNet, a dataset with millions of labeled images.

5
00:00:28,391 --> 00:00:37,991
And in twenty twelve, three researchers from the University of Toronto, Alex Krizhevsky, Ilya Sutskever, and Geoffrey Hinton, enter AlexNet into the competition.

6
00:00:38,141 --> 00:00:44,977
A deep neural network, trained on two gamer graphics cards in Krizhevsky's parents' house.

7
00:00:45,127 --> 00:00:52,764
The result silences the field: an error rate of fifteen point three percent, against twenty-six point two for second place.

8
00:00:52,914 --> 00:00:58,150
Neural networks, the very ones left for dead, had just won.
